The change is in `retire()` in `reef/execution/executor/process_guard.py`, plus the Slime resources code that logs its result. I didn't open either file, so the choice rests on the grep results alone.

The grep for `def retire(` found `retire(token, timeout_s)` only in `process_guard.py`. The other `retire` methods are on unrelated classes (judging, inference residency, recovery, vllm, sglang). The grep for `process_guard` found `reef/train/slime_backend/resources.py` and `reef/train/slime_backend/rollout_group.py` as callers. I put `resources.py` ahead of `rollout_group.py` because the change says "the Slime resources log them". I rank `rollout_group.py` last because I didn't check whether it calls `retire()`.

{"files": ["reef/execution/executor/process_guard.py", "reef/train/slime_backend/resources.py", "reef/train/slime_backend/rollout_group.py"]}

The change belongs in `startup_status` in `reef/service/inference_service.py` (around lines 256–290). I read that function but haven't edited anything.

It reads the current serving request for the scenario and turns that request's state into the model's status. A model that sits beside the active one gets the whole request's `starting` or `failed` state. The fix has to check first whether a published route already serves the artifact, and return `ready` if so. That check can use the existing `resolve` method or the cache. I did not trace how `resolve` looks up routes, so I don't know whether `served_models.py` needs a change too. It is the less likely second file.

{"files": ["reef/service/inference_service.py", "reef/storage/served_models.py"]}

The per-record lookup is in `reef/scenario/factory.py`. During recovery it calls `backend.index_snapshot` once per record (lines 393 and 401), and the Git backend does one `ls-remote` and one fetch for each call. The fix needs a batch version of `index_snapshot`. That version belongs in the Git backend (`git_lfs.py`), on the `RepositoryBackend` base (`repository.py`), and in the in-memory backend (`memory.py`). `factory.py` would then call it once for the whole history.

I haven't read the `remember_selection` path in `repository.py`, so it may also resolve one binding per record. That would be a further place to change.

{"files": ["reef/artifact/git_lfs.py", "reef/artifact/repository.py", "reef/scenario/factory.py", "reef/artifact/memory.py"]}

I've traced the evaluation path and I'm about to finish. `ArtifactEndpoint.answer` calls the runtime's handler directly. It never applies the scenario's serving config, which carries the sampling values. The rollout worker also opens the runtime in its own process, not in the process that started the engine.

I didn't open `reef/inference/tinker.py` (the only runtime that serves artifacts on request), so it is a lower-confidence fifth pick. I'm confident in the first three files.

{"files": ["reef/evaluation/artifact_endpoint.py", "reef/evaluation/rollout_worker.py", "reef/dispatcher/dispatcher.py", "reef/inference/interfaces.py", "reef/inference/tinker.py"]}

The change is in `Scenario.prepare_training_step` in `reef/scenario/scenario.py`. Lines 350–357 return early when the selected artifact does not serve. That check should apply only to automatic training, which is the mode that uses the staleness check.

I did not open `reef/train/trainer.py` beyond a search. It exposes `training_mode` through `pipeline.training_mode`, which may need a public accessor on `Trainer`. I think it is the less likely second file.

{"files": ["reef/scenario/scenario.py", "reef/train/trainer.py"]}

The fix is in `reef/train/pipeline.py`. Both `check_request` (line 493) and `ready` (line 575) go through `request_entries` (line 468). That method calls `self.trainable(stored)` for every sample and keeps all of the results in `entries`. The change would make it check the samples in batches and keep only what it needs.

I did not read `trainable` (line 407) or `reef/storage/samples.py`, where `get` is defined. Either could also need a change if the batching goes into the store.

{"files": ["reef/train/pipeline.py", "reef/storage/samples.py"]}

I couldn't find an existing "hold" in the code. My best guess is that it maps onto the scenario-to-split "use" of an item. Today that use is one split per scenario. `check_scenarios` in `reef/storage/data.py` types it as `dict[str, str]`. `sql_data.py` keeps one row per (owner, item, scenario), and it replaces the row when the split changes. Holding a benchmark in both splits means changing that key to include the split, with a migration for existing rows.

I'm unsure about the exact scope. The `(scenario, kind, name, split)` wording doesn't match any name in `reef/storage`. I haven't read `reef/storage/migrations.py` or `reef/service/routes/data.py` closely, so those two are lower confidence.

{"files": ["reef/storage/data.py", "reef/storage/sql_data.py", "reef/storage/migrations.py", "reef/service/routes/data.py", "reef/storage/tasks.py"]}

This checkout doesn't contain the retry code the change describes, so my list is a best guess. A search of `reef/` for `max_concurrent_rollouts`, `waits_for_retry` and a retry path for evaluations found nothing. The only match for the limit is in `tests/harness/test_task_rollout_runner.py`.

The closest code is in `reef/service/artifact_evaluations.py`, which holds the evaluation `settle` logic and `rollouts_json`. It is where a retry that queues rollouts and the `waits_for_retry` mark would most likely go. `reef/service/routes/evaluations.py` serves the rollouts route that would show a waiting rollout. I did not check where the evaluation settings are defined or where the work item metadata type lives. The two remaining entries are guesses: `reef/evaluation/rollout_worker.py` handles the rollouts and `reef/dispatcher/served.py` has `retry_delay_seconds`.

{"files": ["reef/service/artifact_evaluations.py", "reef/service/routes/evaluations.py", "reef/evaluation/rollout_worker.py", "reef/dispatcher/served.py"]}

The change most likely sits in the lock code in `reef/storage/sql_data.py`. `PostgresDataStore.lock_creates` takes one advisory lock per schema. I did not read the files beyond what `locate` returned.

The lock is called from the abstract `DataStore` in `reef/storage/data.py`, so the lock method's signature probably needs a scenario argument there. The callers that would pass the scenario are likely in `reef/service/data.py` (`store_items`, `delete_item`) and `reef/storage/samples.py`.

